T=D \left(\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\right)^{3$ / 2}
\end{array}

Acceleration due to gravity on the surface of the planet is $g_{p}=\frac{G M_p^{2}}{R_p^{2}}$ Given, $\quad \mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{e}}}{2}$ and $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{e}}}{2}
\therefore \quad g_{p}=\frac{G(M e / 2)}{(\operatorname{Re} / 2)^{2}}=\frac{2 G M_{e}}{R_{e}}=2 g_{e}
$

Given, a space station is at a height equal to radius of Earth, \( \frac{1}{2}M v_s^{2}=\frac{G M m}{(R+h)}\)Here, \( h=R \) and \( v_{s} \) is velocity of space station\[
 \begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow\frac{1}{2}M v_s^{2}=\frac{G M m}{2 R}\\
\Rightarrow v_{s}=\sqrt{\frac{G M}{R}}
\end{array}$\]Now, we know that escape velocity is\[
 \begin{array}{l}v_{e}=\sqrt{\frac{2 G M}{R}}=\sqrt{2} \sqrt{\frac{G M}{2}}\\
\Rightarrow v_{e}=\sqrt{2}v_{s} \\
\text{ or }v_{s}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}v_{e}
\end{array}$
\]
